# Oracle corpus

Expected findings decided by reading the rule descriptions and the source,
before and independently of what the tool reports. `expected.yaml` is written
by hand. Nothing in this directory may be generated from tool output — that
would reproduce the failure it exists to catch.

The rest of the suite compares the tool against a second reading built from
the same inference: the fixture tests assert what the rule was written to do,
and `docs/precision/verify.py` re-implements each predicate. Both have missed
defects that shared their blind spot. `verify.py` could not see rule F's
nested multiply-add because its own predicate also required a named product,
and it had to be extended alongside the rule.

A hand-decided expectation cannot acquire the implementation's blind spot. It
can be wrong — a human decided it — but it is wrong independently, which is
the property the rest of the evidence base does not have.

## Adding a case

1. Write the C file. Keep it small enough to reason about completely.
   Then read the line numbers off the file rather than counting them in
   your head — three of the first six expectations written here had the
   line wrong and nothing else, which is noise the oracle should not be
   spending its failures on.
2. Decide the expected findings from the codebook and the rule's stated
   mechanism. Do not run the tool first.
3. Add the entry to `expected.yaml`, with `why` naming the reasoning.
4. Run the suite. If the tool disagrees, decide which is wrong on the merits
   before changing either.

An empty `findings` list is a claim, not an omission: it says this file
contains no instance of any mechanism.

Only the keys the runner checks may appear — `why` and `findings` on a case,
and `line`, `type`, `rule`, `evidence`, `reason`, `intrinsic`, `suggestion`,
`rationale_includes`, `rationale_excludes` on a finding. A test enforces that.
Before it did, an unrecognised key was skipped, so `evidance: A` was
indistinguishable from a satisfied `evidence` and four deliberate
falsifications left the corpus green. A corpus whose own vocabulary is
unchecked can fail silently, which is the failure it is here to prevent.
# Hand-decided expectations. Never generated from tool output — see README.md.
#
# Each case names the file, why the expectation is what it is, and the exact
# findings expected. An empty list is a claim that the file contains no
# instance of any mechanism, not an omission.
#
# Fields checked when present: line, type, rule, evidence, reason, intrinsic,
# suggestion, rationale_includes, rationale_excludes. Anything not named is
# not asserted, so a case pins what it reasoned about and no more — but a key
# outside that list is a mistake, not a silent no-op, and a test says so.

exclusive_arms.c:
  why: >-
    Both shapes are split across arms of an `if`. In `widening_pair_...` no
    execution reaches the unpack carrying both products, so there is no
    round-trip: rule W's mechanism is one multiply pair feeding one unpack,
    and here only one of the pair ever runs. In `compare_and_consumer_...`
    the compare and the `and` are in opposite arms, so the compare is never
    followed by that consumer at all — rule P's mechanism is a compare whose
    result the next call consumes, and no execution has both. Adjacency in
    source text is not adjacency in any run. Rule M was corrected for this
    same reason and rule F after it; the mechanism definitions are what
    decide it, not which rules happen to ask.
  findings: []

exclusive_arms_control.c:
  why: >-
    The same two shapes with no branching, so both are instances. The
    widening round-trip is one finding, not two: the mechanism is the
    round-trip, and the rule anchors it at the low multiply. Both its
    operands are plain variables resolved in the same unit, so nothing is
    unresolved and the grade is A. The compare's result is consumed by the
    very next call, which is rule P's mechanism exactly; P grades A always.
  findings:
    - line: 5
      type: W
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi16
    - line: 12
      type: P
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_cmpgt_epi32

portable_fallback.c:
  why: >-
    A multiply-add is present and unfused, so a type F finding belongs here.
    What must not appear is a claim about emitted machine instructions:
    `_mm256_mullo_epi16` has no NEON branch in SIMDe 0.8.4 and falls through
    to a portable loop, so the SIMDe source does not say what the compiler
    produces. The knowledge entry concedes this by recording both instruction
    counts as unknown; the rationale has to concede it too. `vmlaq_s16` is
    still the right fused form to name, and for a 256-bit value it applies to
    each 128-bit half — which the `suggestion` field says as well as the
    rationale, so a JSON consumer and a text reader get one claim.

    The second function is the same intrinsic against a 32-lane accumulator,
    where the recorded instruction does not fit and the suggestion is
    withdrawn. The concession about what is emitted does not depend on the
    width: there is no NEON branch either way, so that path must not assert
    separate instructions while the matching-width path declines to.
  findings:
    - line: 8
      type: F
      intrinsic: _mm256_mullo_epi16
      suggestion: vmlaq_s16 per 128-bit half
      rationale_excludes: emitted as separate instructions
      rationale_includes: per 128-bit half
    - line: 16
      type: F
      evidence: C
      reason: transform_width_mismatch
      intrinsic: _mm256_mullo_epi16
      suggestion: null
      rationale_excludes: emitted as separate instructions

redundant_zero_init.c:
  why: >-
    Rule R matches calls to the intrinsics registered in redundant.yaml and
    never inspects the consumer, so each registered call is one finding and
    the grade is always C. The reason is the one the rule can honestly give:
    the zero-init is dead only for a consumer it cannot see. `_mm_loadu_si128`
    loads a full vector, so there is no zero-init for the expansion to make
    redundant and nothing to report.
  findings:
    - line: 7
      type: R
      evidence: C
      reason: transform_requires_context
      intrinsic: _mm_loadu_si32
    - line: 8
      type: R
      evidence: C
      reason: transform_requires_context
      intrinsic: _mm_loadl_epi64

scalar_assembly.c:
  why: >-
    Type M's two mechanisms. Three inserts on one target meet the default
    threshold of three and are one finding, not three — the mechanism is the
    chain. Two inserts are below it and are nothing. A `_mm_set_epi32` over
    runtime scalars is the second mechanism; the same call over literals is a
    constant vector and is excluded by the rule's own description. The line a
    chain anchors at is not something the published description fixes, so it
    is deliberately not asserted here.
  findings:
    - type: M
      rule: M.scalar_insert_chain
    - line: 21
      type: M
      rule: M.scalar_set_build
      intrinsic: _mm_set_epi32
    - line: 34
      type: M
      rule: M.scalar_insert_chain
      intrinsic: _mm256_insert_epi64

shuffle_guard.c:
  why: >-
    Rule S matches every `_mm_shuffle_epi8` and grades on whether the mask's
    lanes are known to be safe, so both calls are findings and the grades
    differ. The first mask is sixteen literal indices, all inside 0..15,
    where pshufb and tbl agree — nothing is unknown and nothing needs a
    guard, so grade A. The second mask is loaded at runtime: no lane can be
    read from the source, which is unresolved rather than a guard the rule
    confirmed is load-bearing.
  findings:
    - line: 9
      type: S
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_shuffle_epi8
    - line: 16
      type: S
      evidence: C
      reason: unresolved
      intrinsic: _mm_shuffle_epi8

fusion_shapes.c:
  why: >-
    Rule F's mechanism is a multiply whose product an add consumes instead of
    fusing, and whether the author named the product is a spelling choice —
    both spellings are the same instance and both are findings. `mullo_epi32`
    produces 32-bit lanes accumulated by a 32-bit add, which `vmlaq_s32`
    matches, so nothing is withheld and the grade is A. One add is one fusion
    opportunity, so the pair of products at line 18 is one finding, not two.
    A multiply with no add is no instance at all. The float pair is the
    mechanism too, but `vfmaq_f32` rounds once where the separate multiply and
    add round twice, so it is never an exact substitution — grade C, and the
    instruction is still named because it exists and has a cost, not because
    it can be applied freely.
  findings:
    - line: 6
      type: F
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi32
      suggestion: vmlaq_s32
    - line: 12
      type: F
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi32
      suggestion: vmlaq_s32
    - line: 18
      type: F
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi32
      suggestion: vmlaq_s32
    - line: 30
      type: F
      evidence: C
      reason: transform_changes_result
      intrinsic: _mm_mul_ps
      suggestion: vfmaq_f32

nested_regions.c:
  why: >-
    The control-region test exists to reject arms of an `if` that cannot both
    execute. Nesting is a different relation: where one region encloses the
    other, any path reaching the inner one has already run, or will run, the
    outer. All three of these are real instances. In the first the add is
    inside the `if` and consumes the product whenever the branch is taken; in
    the second the multiply is inside and the add after it consumes whatever
    the name holds, which on the taken path is that product; in the third both
    multiplies sit together and only the unpack is nested. Rejecting these
    would trade one false-positive class for a false-negative one, and VVenC
    has six of exactly this shape.
  findings:
    - line: 7
      type: F
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi32
    - line: 17
      type: F
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi32
    - line: 24
      type: W
      evidence: A
      intrinsic: _mm_mullo_epi16
"""The tool against expectations decided independently of it.

Every other test in this suite, and `docs/precision/verify.py` too, compares
the tool against a second reading built from the same inference. Both have
missed defects that shared their blind spot: `verify.py` could not see rule
F's nested multiply-add because its own predicate also required a named
product, and had to be extended alongside the rule.

`tests/oracle/expected.yaml` is written by hand from the rule descriptions
and the source, before the tool is run. It can be wrong, but it is wrong
independently — which is the property nothing else here has.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from pathlib import Path

import pytest
import yaml

from simde_lint.analyze import analyze

ORACLE = Path(__file__).parent / "oracle"
CASES = ORACLE / "cases"

_CHECKED = ("line", "type", "rule", "evidence", "reason", "intrinsic", "suggestion")
_RATIONALE = ("rationale_includes", "rationale_excludes")
_ALLOWED_IN_A_FINDING = frozenset(_CHECKED) | frozenset(_RATIONALE)
_ALLOWED_IN_A_CASE = frozenset({"why", "findings"})


def _expected() -> dict:
    return yaml.safe_load((ORACLE / "expected.yaml").read_text())


def _actual(path: Path) -> list:
    findings, _, _ = analyze([path])
    return sorted(findings, key=lambda f: (f.line, f.type, f.intrinsic))


def _describe(finding) -> str:
    return (
        f"line {finding.line} {finding.type} {finding.evidence.value} "
        f"{finding.intrinsic}"
    )


@pytest.mark.parametrize("case", sorted(_expected()))
def test_an_expectation_only_uses_fields_this_runner_checks(case):
    """An unrecognised key was skipped, so a typo passed as agreement.

    The comparison below reads the fields it knows and ignores the rest, which
    made `evidance: A` indistinguishable from a satisfied `evidence`. Four such
    typos left the whole corpus green. That failure mode is silence, which is
    what every defect this corpus exists to catch has looked like -- and it
    applied to the only test pinning the portable-fallback rationale.

    Checked in its own test rather than inside the comparison: a malformed
    expectation is a defect in the expectation, and saying so separately keeps
    it from reading as a disagreement with the tool.
    """
    expectation = _expected()[case]
    unknown = set(expectation) - _ALLOWED_IN_A_CASE
    assert not unknown, f"{case}: unrecognised key(s) {sorted(unknown)}"
    for want in expectation["findings"]:
        unknown = set(want) - _ALLOWED_IN_A_FINDING
        assert not unknown, (
            f"{case}: unrecognised key(s) {sorted(unknown)} in an expected "
            "finding -- nothing would check them"
        )


@pytest.mark.parametrize("case", sorted(_expected()))
def test_the_tool_agrees_with_the_hand_decided_expectation(case):
    expectation = _expected()[case]
    assert expectation.get("why"), f"{case}: an expectation needs its reasoning"
    findings = _actual(CASES / case)
    wanted = expectation["findings"]

    assert len(findings) == len(wanted), (
        f"{case}: expected {len(wanted)} findings, got {len(findings)}: "
        + "; ".join(_describe(f) for f in findings)
    )

    # Both sides sort on the same key, and a `line`-less expectation is
    # matched by position among the findings rather than forced to the front.
    # Sorting expectations by `line` with a 0 default put such an entry first
    # whatever its mechanism anchors at, so a case passed only because its
    # chain happened to be the earliest of three findings.
    keyed = [w for w in wanted if "line" in w]
    unkeyed = [w for w in wanted if "line" not in w]
    order = sorted(keyed, key=lambda w: (w["line"], w.get("type", ""), w.get("intrinsic", "")))
    if unkeyed:
        # Place each in the gap its neighbours leave: with keys present the
        # ordering is total, so an unkeyed entry can only take a remaining
        # slot. Zip below pairs them in finding order.
        remaining = [f for f in findings if not any(w.get("line") == f.line for w in keyed)]
        assert len(remaining) == len(unkeyed), (
            f"{case}: {len(unkeyed)} expectation(s) without a line, but "
            f"{len(remaining)} finding(s) unaccounted for"
        )
        order = sorted(
            order + unkeyed,
            key=lambda w: w.get("line", remaining[unkeyed.index(w)].line)
            if w in unkeyed else w["line"],
        )
    for finding, want in zip(findings, order):
        for field in _CHECKED:
            if field not in want:
                continue
            actual = getattr(finding, field)
            if hasattr(actual, "value"):
                actual = actual.value
            assert actual == want[field], (
                f"{case} line {finding.line}: {field} is {actual!r}, "
                f"expected {want[field]!r}"
            )
        if "rationale_includes" in want:
            assert want["rationale_includes"] in finding.rationale, (
                f"{case} line {finding.line}: rationale does not say "
                f"{want['rationale_includes']!r} — {finding.rationale}"
            )
        if "rationale_excludes" in want:
            assert want["rationale_excludes"] not in finding.rationale, (
                f"{case} line {finding.line}: rationale still says "
                f"{want['rationale_excludes']!r} — {finding.rationale}"
            )


def test_every_case_file_has_an_expectation():
    # A .c file with no entry would be scanned by nothing and look like
    # coverage. The oracle is only as good as its completeness.
    cases = {
        path.name
        for path in CASES.iterdir()
        if path.suffix in {".c", ".cc", ".cpp", ".h", ".hpp"}
    }
    assert cases == set(_expected())

