The oracle’s independence claim is too strong: writing expectations by hand before running the tool does not prevent shared assumptions. Its runner also cannot assert costs. I found a further boundary worth checking: rule W’s description says two unpacks produce two findings, but its matching loop appears to consume the multiply pair after the first.
